ing because he's just dividing the total by the capacity per page. So, the answer should be 42. Let me just make sure there's no trick here. The problem doesn't mention anything about the pages needing to be labeled, or needing something extra, like maybe recycling or something. It just says he wants to put these cards in special pages. So, dividing gives the correct answer. \n\nYep, I think that's it. The answer is 42.\n</think>\n\nPuncher collects 60 packs of baseball cards, with each pack containing 7 cards. To find the total number of cards, we multiply these two quantities:\n\n$$\n60 \\text{ packs} \\times 7 \\text{ cards per pack} = 420 \\text{ cards}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to determine how many pages are required to store these 420 cards, knowing each page can hold 10 cards:\n\n$$\n\\frac{420 \\text{ cards}}{10 \\text{ cards per page}} = 42 \\text{ pages}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Punger needs to buy $\\boxed{42}$ pages.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["42"], "solution": "42"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30436, 1389, 51207, 19507, 7411, 13, 220, 1260, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 