creases by 10 feet in June, then by 3 times that in July. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is saying that from mid-May to mid-June is a one-month increase, then mid-June to mid-July is another month. So the depth increases by 10 feet in June, then by 3 times that in July. So 15 feet * 3 = 45 feet. \n\nHmm, so that seems straightforward. Let me verify once more. \n\nMid-May: 5 feet. \n\nMid-June: 5 + 10 = 15 feet. \n\nMid-July: 15 feet * 3 = 45 feet. \n\nYes, that seems right. Maybe another way to think about it is adding 10 feet to mid-May and then multiplying by three, which gives 5 + 10 = 15, times 3 is 45. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. So the river was 45 feet deep at mid-July.\n</think>\n\nWe are given that:\n\n- In Mid-May, the river is 5 feet deep.\n- In Mid-June, the river is 10 feet deeper than in Mid-May.\n- In Mid-July, the river is three times as deep as in Mid-June.\n\nLet's calculate the depth of the river by mid-July step by step:\n\n1. Mid-May depth: **5 feet**\n2. Mid-June depth: Since the river is 10 feet deeper than mid-May, we add 10 feet:\n   $$\n   5 + 10 = 15 \\text{ feet}\n   $$\n3. Mid-July depth: The river is three times deeper than mid-June, so we multiply the mid-June depth by 3:\n   $$\n   15 \\times 3 = 45 \\text{ feet}\n   $$\n\nThus, the total depth of the river by mid-July is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{45}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "In mid-May, the river flowing through Moreland is five feet deep. By mid-June, the river is 10 feet deeper than mid-May. By mid-July, the river is three times deeper than mid-June. How many feet deep is the river by mid-July?", "answer": "In mid-June, the river is 5+10=<<5+10=15>>15 feet deep.\nIn mid-July, the river is 15*3=<<15*3=45>>45 feet deep.\n#### 45", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: In mid-May, the river flowing through Moreland is five feet deep. By mid-June, the river is 10