ip, and the tax is also 25%, then total cost would be 20 (food before tip) plus tip (5) plus tax (already included?), but that seems inconsistent. Maybe the tax was part of the tip? Or is tax separate?\n\nWait, maybe the problem is written as \"she left a 25% tip\" and \"with tax\", so perhaps tax is the 25% she left. But that would mean that there is a total of two 25% charges: tip and tax? In that case, total cost would be 20 + 5 + 5 = 30? But the problem says \"with tax\", which would imply that tax is added. But maybe the tax is already 25% of the cost, so total is 20 + tip (5) + tax (5) = 30? That seems possible. But which one is it?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tax is the 25% tip, which is already included in the food cost. So total cost is 20 + 5 = 25? Then total with tax would be 25 dollars? That seems high. But maybe that's how it's supposed to be. Let me check the problem again:\n\nProblem: She left a 25% tip. How much did the meal cost Hilary, with tax, in dollars?\n\nDoes \"with tax\" imply that tax is already included? Usually, people add tax before calculating the total. So if tax is 25%, then teacher's answer is total between food cost and tax. But if the tip is 25%, and tax is 25%, then total is 20 + tip (5) + tax (5) = 30. However, the problem says \"with tax\", so maybe tax is separate. Therefore, maybe the answer is 20 + 5 = 25, with tax as 25%? But I need to check if tax is already included. Wait, usually, tax is a separate category, so she would add tax here.\n\nBut the problem says \"she left a 25% tip. How much did the meal cost Hilary, with tax, in dollars?\"\n\nSo \"with tax\" would mean that tax is included. Therefore, total cost is the sum of food, tip, and tax. So first calculate food, then add tip, then add tax? But the tax is already defined as 25% on total cost? So if tip is also 25%, then:\n\nTotal food cost before tip: 20\n\nTip: 25% of total cost = 20*(0.25) = 5\n\nTax: 25% of total cost = 5\n\nTotal cost with tax: 20 + 5 + 5 = 30.\n