t me re-read that.\n\nThe problem says: \"It takes 4 bananas to make one cup of mush.\" So that translates to 1 cup of mush requires 4 bananas. Therefore, if she uses 20 bananas, the number of cups of mush she can make is 20 bananas divided by 4 bananas per cup, which would be 5 cups of mush. Wait, is that right? So 20 divided by 4 equals 5. Yes, that seems correct.\n\nSo, if she makes 5 cups of mush, then according to the flour-to-mush ratio, she needs 3 cups of flour per 1 cup of mush. Therefore, 5 cups of flour multiplied by 3 gives her how much flour? 5 times 3 is 15. So she would need 15 cups of flour?\n\nLet me confirm again. Let's lay out the steps:\n\n1. Calculate how many cups of mush she makes with 20 bananas.\n   - Since 4 bananas = 1 cup of mush, then 20 bananas / 4 bananas per cup = 5 cups of mush.\n\n2. Then, calculate flour needed per cup of mush: 3 cups of flour / 1 cup of mush = 3 cups of flour per cup of mush.\n\n3. Multiply the cups of mush by the flour per cup to get total flour needed.\n   - 5 cups of mush * 3 cups of flour per cup = 15 cups of flour.\n\nSo, the answer should be 15 cups of flour. Therefore, written as \\boxed{15} in the box.\n\nWait, let me check once more for errors. Did I read the problem correctly? Yes. The problem says she needs 3 cups of flour for each cup of banana mush. So that ratio is 3:1. So, if she uses 5 cups of mush, then 3*5=15 cups of flour. Yep, that seems right. I don't see any mistakes here. The steps make sense, so the answer should be 15.\n</think>\n\n$$\n\\boxed{15}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Hannah is making banana bread. She needs to use 3 cups of flour for every cup of banana mush. It takes 4 bananas to make one cup of mush. If Hannah uses 20 bananas, how many cups of flour should she use?", "answer": "First find the number of cups of banana mush Hannah gets: 20 bananas / 4 bananas/cup = <<20/4=5>>5 cups\nThen multiply the number of cups of mush by the ratio of flour to mush to find th