2026-10-30 08:13:17 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 44, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Nicki spent the last year running a lot. For the first half of the year, she ran a total of 20 miles per week. For the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week. How many miles total did she run for the year?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Nicki spent the last year running a lot. For the first half of the year, she ran a total of 20 miles per week. For the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week. How many miles total did she run for the year?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Nicki ran a lot during the year. The problem says she spent the last year, so that's two weeks of running, right? Wait, actually, wait. Wait, the problem says for the first half and the second half of the year. If the year is divided into two equal halves, then each half would be a half of a year. But how many weeks are in a year? A full year has 52 weeks, right? Let me confirm. Yes, a year is typically 52 weeks. \n\nSo, for the first half of the year, she ran 20 miles per week. If there are 52 weeks in a year, then the total for the first half would be 20 miles/week multiplied by 52 weeks. Then for the second half, she increased mileage to 30 miles per week. So total miles for the second half would be 30 miles/week multiplied by the same 52 weeks again. Then add both together to get the total miles for the year.\n\nLet me check if I got the problem right. The problem says \"the last year running a lot.\" So the split into half, and each half being the first half and the second half of the year. So if a year is split into two halves, each half has 52 weeks, so 20+30 and 20+30 Again, so 20*52 + 30*52. \n\nSo total miles would be (20 + 30)*52. Let's compute that. 20+30 is 50, times 52. So 50*52. Let me calculate that. 50*50 is 2500, plus 50*2 is 100, so total 2600. Therefore, the answer is 2600 miles in total.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Let me do the multiplication another way. 50 times 52. Well, 50 times 50 is 2500, and 50 times 2 is 100, so 2500+100=2600. Yes, same result. Therefore, the answer is \\boxed{2600}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{2600}\n</think>\n\nTo solve Nicki's total running mileage for the year, we start by determining the number of total weeks in the year. A full year typically has 52 weeks, so each half of the year has 52 weeks.\n\nFor the first half of the year, Nicki ran 20 miles per week, so the total miles for the first half would be $20 \\times 52 = 1040$ miles.\n\nFor the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week, leading to a total of $30 \\times 52 = 1560$ miles.\n\nAdding both halves together, the total miles Nicki ran for the year is:\n$$ 1040 + 1560 = 2600 $$\n\nThus, the total distance Nicki ran for the year is $\\boxed{2600}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Nicki spent the last year running a lot. For the first half of the year, she ran a total of 20 miles per week. For the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week. How many miles total did she run for the year?", "answer": "Each half of the year consists of 52 / 2 = <<52/2=26>>26 weeks.\nFor the first half of the year, Nicki ran a total of 20 * 26 = <<20*26=520>>520 miles.\nFor the second half of the year, Nicki ran a total of 30 * 26 = <<30*26=780>>780 miles.\nFor the entire year, Nicki ran a total of 520 + 780 = <<520+780=1300>>1,300 miles.\n#### 1300", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Nicki spent the last year running a lot. For the first half of the year, she ran a total of 20 miles per week. For the second half of the year, she increased the mileage to 30 miles per week. How many miles total did she run for the year?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["1300"], "solution": "1300"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 14991, 72, 7391, 279, 1537, 1042, 4303, 264, 2696, 13, 1752, 279, 1156, 4279, 315, 279, 1042, 11, 1340, 10613, 264, 2790, 315, 220, 17, 15, 8756, 817, 2003, 13, 1752, 279, 2086, 4279, 315, 279, 1042], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 14991, 72, 10613, 264, 2696, 2337, 279, 1042, 13, 576, 3491, 2727, 1340, 7391, 279, 1537, 1042, 11, 773, 429, 594, 1378, 5555, 315, 4303, 11, 1290, 30, 13824, 11, 3520, 11, 3783, 13, 13824, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 369, 279, 1156, 4279, 323, 279, 2086, 4279, 315, 279, 1042, 13, 1416, 279, 1042, 374, 17779]}
