0}.\n\nBut let me check again. Let me try variables. Let T be the total amount borrowed. She returned half of that now, so she has T/2. She borrowed T, so she has T/2. Then, over 10 months (6+4), she would have paid $10 * 10 = $100. Therefore, T/2 = 100? Wait, that would mean T = 200. Therefore, she needs to pay back $100 in full from now, so she will still owe $100. So answer is $100. Which in boxed is \\boxed{100}.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another approach: Since she returns $10 each month, in 6 months she has returned $60. Now, she returns half, so say the total amount was $200, so she returned 100, leaving half. Therefore, she still owes 100. Therefore, the answer is \\boxed{100}.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{100}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Shara borrowed money from her brother 6 months ago. She returned $10 per month to her brother. Currently, she was able to return half of the money she borrowed. How much will she still owe her brother 4 months from now?", "answer": "For 6 months, Shara was able to return $10 x 6 = $<<10*6=60>>60 to her brother.\nSince she already returned half of the money, then this means she currently owes her brother $<<60=60>>60.\nFour months from now, she will be able to pay an additional $10 x 4 = $<<10*4=40>>40.\nThus, she will still owe her brother $60 - $40 = $<<60-40=20>>20 four months from now.\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Shara borrowed money from her brother 6 months ago. She returned $10 per month to her brother. Currently, she was able to return half of the money she borrowed. How much will she still owe her brother 4 months from now?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 1417, 5059, 45564, 3220, 504, 1059, 10641, 220, 21, 3951