2026-10-30 08:25:17 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 57, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. There are five food companies donating food items. I need to figure out the total number of items they all donated. Let me start by breaking down each company's donations step by step.\n\nFirst, Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens. That's straightforward. Now, American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the dressed chickens from Foster Farms. Since Foster Farms had 45, then American Summits should have 45 multiplied by 2. Let me check that... 45x2 is 90. So American Summits donated 90 bottles of water.\n\nNext, Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated. Foster Farms has 45, so Hormel has 45 times 3. 45x3 is 135. So Hormel donated 135 dressed chickens.\n\nNow, Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated. Hormel's number was 135, so one-third of that would be 135 divided by 3. Let me calculate that. 135 divided by 3 is 45. So Boudin Butchers donated 45 dressed chickens.\n\nLastly, Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. American Summits had 90 bottles of water, so Del Monte's would be 90 minus 30. That's 60 bottles of water.\n\nNow, let me sum all these numbers up. Foster Farms: 45, American Summits:90, Hormel:135, Boudin:45, Del Monte:60. Let me add them step by step. \n\nFirst, 45 plus 90 equals 135. Then plus 135, that's 270. Then plus 45, that totals 315. Finally, plus 60, so 315 + 60 = 375. \n\nSo the total number of food items donated by all five companies is 375. \n\nWait, let me check if I added them correctly. Let me re-add again:\n\nFoster: 45\n\nAmerican: 90\n\nHormel: 135\n\nBoudin: 45\n\nDel Monte: 60\n\nTotal: 45 + 90 is 135; 135 +135 is 270; 270 +45 is 315; 315 +60 is 375. Yes, that's correct. So the answer should be \\boxed{375}.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{375}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Five food companies sponsored a local food bank. Foster Farms donated 45 dressed chickens; American Summits donated twice the number of bottled water than the number of dressed chicken donated by Foster Farms; Hormel donated three times the number of dressed chickens that Foster Farms donated; Boudin Butchers donated one-third of the number of dressed chickens that Hormel donated; Del Monte Foods donated 30 fewer bottles of water than American Summits. How many food items did the companies donate in total?",