ach watermelon contributes its own number of slices based on the cut size, so multiplying by the number of watermelons and then adding for each family member.\n</think>\n\nThe total number of watermelon slices is calculated by multiplying the number of watermelons each person brought by the number of slices per watermelon and adding the slices from the sister's watermelon. \n\nDanny's 3 watermelons each divided into 10 slices: $3 \\times 10 = 30$ slices.  \nHis sister's 1 watermelon divided into 15 slices: $1 \\times 15 = 15$ slices.  \nTotal slices: $30 + 15 = \\boxed{45}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Danny brings 3 watermelons to his family picnic. He cuts each watermelon into 10 slices. His sister brings 1 watermelon to the family picnic, and she cuts the watermelon into 15 slices. How many watermelon slices are there in total at the picnic?", "answer": "From Danny, there are 3 * 10 = <<3*10=30>>30 watermelon slices\nFrom his sister, there are 1 * 15 = <<1*15=15>>15 watermelon slices\nThere are a total of 30 + 15 = <<30+15=45>>45 watermelon slices\n#### 45", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Danny brings 3 watermelons to his family picnic. He cuts each watermelon into 10 slices. His sister brings 1 watermelon to the family picnic, and she cuts the watermelon into 15 slices. How many watermelon slices are there in total at the picnic?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["45"], "solution": "45"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 32599, 12434, 220, 18, 3015, 27127, 2382, 311, 806, 2997, 54462, 13, 1260, 15104, 1817, 3015, 71118, 1119, 220, 16, 15, 34254, 13, 5301, 12923, 12434, 220, 16, 3015, 71118, 311, 279, 2997, 54462, 11, 323, 1340], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 32599, 7117, 220, 18, 3015, 