 the box.\n</think>\n\nThe problem involves calculating the total length of wood needed for two types: 6 lengths of 4 feet each and 2 lengths of 2 feet each. \n\n1. Calculate the total length from the 4-foot lengths: \n   $$\n   6 \\text{ (lengths)} \\times 4 \\text{ feet (length)} = 24 \\text{ feet}\n   $$\n\n2. Calculate the total length from the 2-foot lengths: \n   $$\n   2 \\text{ (lengths)} \\times 2 \\text{ feet (length)} = 4 \\text{ feet}\n   $$\n\n3. Add the two total lengths together: \n   $$\n   24 \\text{ feet} + 4 \\text{ feet} = 28 \\text{ feet}\n   $$\n\nThus, the total length of wood Steve needs to buy is $\\boxed{28}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Steve is building a bench for the playground and needs 6 lengths of wood that measure 4 feet and 2 lengths of wood that measure 2 feet. How many feet of wood does Steve need to buy?", "answer": "Steve needs 6 lengths of 4 feet of wood or 6 x 4 = <<6*4=24>>24 feet of wood.\nHe also needs 2 lengths of 2 feet of wood or 2 x 2 = <<2*2=4>>4 feet of wood.\nAltogether, Steve needs 24 feet of wood + 4 feet of wood = <<24+4=28>>28 feet of wood.\n#### 28", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Steve is building a bench for the playground and needs 6 lengths of wood that measure 4 feet and 2 lengths of wood that measure 2 feet. How many feet of wood does Steve need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["28"], "solution": "28"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 13809, 374, 4752, 264, 13425, 369, 279, 41615, 323, 3880, 220, 21, 28316, 315, 7579, 429, 6629, 220, 19, 7541, 323, 220, 17, 28316, 315, 7579, 429, 6629, 220, 17, 7541, 13, 2585, 1657, 7541, 315, 7579], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 71486, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 13809, 3880, 311, 1936, 264, 13425, 11, 323, 566, 3880, 2