rt pint. And five trays of jumbo shrimp is 5*$5, which is 25 dollars. \n\nAdding those together: 5P (frozen yogurt) + 2*(P/2) (chewing gum) + 25 (shrimp trays) = total $55. Let me write that equation:\n\n5P + 2*(P/2) + 25 = 55.\n\nLet me simplify the equation step by step. First, 2*(P/2) is just P, right? Because multiplying by 2 and dividing by 2 cancels out. So the equation becomes:\n\n5P + P + 25 = 55.\n\nCombining like terms: 5P + P is 6P, so 6P + 25 = 55. \n\nSubtract 25 from both sides: 6P = 30. Dividing both sides by 6: P = 5. \n\nWait, so the price of a pint of frozen yogurt is $5? Let me check again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. \n\nFirst, price of jumbo shrimp tray is $5, 5 trays would be 5*5=25. Pints of frozen yogurt were 5, so 5P. Chewing gum is half the price of a pint, so each pack is P/2, two packs would be 2*(P/2)=P. Adding all these: 5P + P +25=6P+25. Set equal to total 55. 6P=30 => P=5. Yes, that seems right. So the answer is $\\boxed{5}$ dollars. And since the question asks for the price of a pint, putting \\boxed{5} as the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe price of a pint of frozen yogurt is $\\boxed{5}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Shannon bought 5 pints of frozen yogurt, two packs of chewing gum, and five trays of jumbo shrimp from The Food Place for a total of  $55 If the price of a tray of jumbo shrimp is $5 and a pack of chewing gum costs half as much as a pint of frozen yogurt, what is the price of a pint of frozen yogurt?", "answer": "5 trays of jumbo shrimp cost 5 x 5 = $<<5*5=25>>25\n5 pints of frozen yogurt and 2 packs of gum in total cost 55 - 25 = $30.\n2 packs of gum cost as much 2 / 2 = <<2/2=1>>1 pint of frozen yogurt\nTherefore 5 + 1 = <<5+1=6>>6 pints of frozen yogurt costs $30.\nA pint of frozen yogurt costs 30 / 6 = $<<30/6=5>>5\n#### 5", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Shannon bought 5 pints of frozen yogurt, two 